>> sourcehut (lists.sr.ht) is the closest match to what you propose. A
>> reply in a patchset thread can carry the header
>> "X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update:" with one of PROPOSED, NEEDS_REVISION,
>> SUPERSEDED, APPROVED, REJECTED or APPLIED. The list sets the
>> patchset's state from it.
>
> Interesting can you provide docs and examples for this? Also how does it
> compare to other methods?
>
>> So the big three keep series state in a web tool, and only use mail
>> for approval trailers. Sourcehut and Git keep it in mail. Your goal
>> of deriving state from the list puts us with them.
>
> I don't mind projecting state in another tool that makes it all more
> visible... but it should be possible to reconstruct it from the mailing
> list and/or git repo.
>
> Essentially there needs to be a database somewhere. That can be the
> corpus of emails, metadata in a git branch, or a dedicated database.
>
>>> Again a goal of this workflow is to have the current state of the list
>>> be derivable from the contents of the list. So I won't tag things as
>>> overcome or accepted - those need to be derived from the
>>> contents. Supporting trailers - or a similar mechanism - lets me add
>>> metadata to emails after the fact.
>>
>> That fits what is already on the list. "[PATCH] notmuch: Tag patches
>> reported committed" [1] adds a header_ids helper to mail-tag.sh. It
>> pulls a Message-ID out of a named header, and the committed tag is
>> derived from it on every notmuch new. Your choice there of a header
>> naming the Message-ID [2] carries over to this. Here is a shape I
>> would propose, for you to decide on:
>>
>> 1. Key names. Use hyphens: Superseded-by, Approved-by, Rejected-by. I
>>    checked "git interpret-trailers --parse". It does not accept
>>    "Overcome by:" with a space, and one invalid line voids the whole
>>    trailer block. I would also say "superseded" rather than
>>    "overcome", since Patchwork and sourcehut both use it, but the name
>>    is yours to pick.
>
> Superseded-by sounds good.
>
>> 2. Header or body trailer. Body trailers are visible and easy to type
>>    in any client, and git interpret-trailers can parse them. They need
>>    care, though. Quoted lines must not count, and a signature after
>>    the trailers stops git from finding them. A header like the
>>    Committed: one is unambiguous, but it is hidden and harder to write
>>    by hand. I lean toward body trailers parsed with git
>>    interpret-trailers, applied only to the unquoted last paragraph.
>
> I think body trailers are the way to go. The idea is that I would reply
> to an email, and the last several lines would be the trailers that I
> introduce.
>
>
> [...]
>
>> 4. Authority. Anyone can write a trailer, including me, so the hook
>>    should count Approved-by and Rejected-by only when the From is a
>>    maintainer address. Superseded-by could also be accepted from the
>>    series' own author. If you want "the latest trailer wins" when two
>>    disagree, ordering by Date works.
>
> For now I think we're okay. And yes the latest trailer wins.
>
>> 5. Supersession without an extra reply. Every revised cover letter
>>    already ends with "Link to v(N-1): id:<msgid>", which
>>    src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md requires. The hook could derive
>>    superseded for the v(N-1) thread from that line. The explicit
>>    Superseded-by reply would then be for cases the Link line does not
>>    cover, such as a series replaced by a different one.
>
